the family of an Arkansas man is suing  the jail he died in the family claims he  was denied adequate medical care  Michael sabi died in 2015 just days  after he was locked up at the bi-state  Justice Center police arrested him over  a verbal dispute with his wife the  following video taken hours before he  died may be disturbing to watch this was  recorded by a jail staffer it appears to  show Savi's heavy breathing and weak  condition as he was taken back to his  cell Omar Villafranca is outside the  jail and Texarkana Texas along the  Arkansas border  Omar good morning good morning Michael  sapis family says that jail knew he had  serious medical conditions and did not  get him help when he needed it Savi's  attorney gave us video from inside the  jail that appears to show his state  about 12 hours before he was found dead  it's worth noting CBS News has not been  able to independently confirm the  video's authenticity surveillance video  given to CBS News from Michael Savi's  attorney appears to show a security  guard at the bi-state Justice Center  throws Abby to the ground according to  the lawsuit  Sabby wasn't feeling well and had  stopped to lean against the wall before  attempting to enter the booking area to  make a phone call a second video taken  by a jail employee purportedly shows  what happened after Savi is on the floor  he's held down by six guards and  pepper-sprayed brought to a jail nurse  for less than a minute rinsed off and  returned to his cell during the nine and  a half minutes video Sabby says he can't  breathe at least 19 times and asked for  water  the next morning jail guards found a 35  year old dead on his jail cell floor  he's a medically vulnerable person so he  reported at at intake that he had  hypertension diabetes heart disease and  asthma Eric Hite is representing Savi's  family in a lawsuit against the jail  filed earlier this week the suit claims  jail staff didn't give sabhi his  medications ignored his labored  breathing and used excessive force  LaSalle Corrections run  this privately-owned Jail and 17 other  facilities across four states they said  they do not make comments on pending  litigation but told a local news station  last October that they comply with the  Texas Jail Commission standards hypeeee  says the family wants justice and  answers for the father of four they want  to expose what happened in the hopes  that this sort of thing doesn't happen  to anyone else according to jail  protocol somebody was supposed to check  on sabhi every 30 minutes overnight the  suit claims that a guard said she did  but then later admitted to lying  Nora really disturbing story Omar thank  you so much
 

